-- hypermath kernel.hm
-- The verification kernel, expressed as a derivation within the system it verifies.
-- A compliant implementation is ~200 lines in any host language.
-- Trust boundary: this file is the spec. The host-language impl is a reader of this spec.
--
-- Key property: verify(kernel.hm) ~ kernel.hm
-- The kernel satisfies itself at ~ level. This is not a filtration-incompleteness paradox
-- (formerly: Gödel paradox; see nomenclature.hm § 4) — it is ax-sim
-- applied to the verify function. Claimed at ~, not at =.
-- Status: FORM at ~ level. FRAME at = level (path coincidence requires external audit).

-- ================================================================
-- § 0. Imports
-- ================================================================

import "axioms.hm"
    -- provides: ground, apply, ~, ≡, =, ax-diff, ax-sim, ax-box, filtration
import "stdlib/surordinal_metric.hm"
    -- provides: d_W metric on W-sep; W-metric topology; ≡-completeness of W-sep
    -- enables: verify(kernel.hm) ≡ kernel.hm as a formal claim (see § 3)

-- ================================================================
-- § 1. The verify function as a □-application
-- ================================================================

-- The kernel IS a function: verify :: HmFile -> RelationResult
-- where RelationResult ∈ { similar | congruent | equal | FRAME(reason) }
--
-- The fundamental claim: verify IS □-application in the meta-layer.
--
--   verify(f) = apply_meta(f)
--
-- Therefore, by ax-sim applied in the meta-layer:
--   apply_meta(f) ~ f   for all f
--
-- This is the self-satisfaction property: the kernel, applied to any .hm file,
-- returns something in the ~-class of that file (preserves continuation capacity
-- at the relation level). It never promotes a ~ derivation to = without evidence.

derive verify-is-sim-map as FORM:
    -- verify preserves similarity class: verify(f) ~ f for all .hm files f.
    step 1: verify is defined as: parse f, check each step's relation stratum,
            return the minimum stratum across all steps.
    step 2: "return minimum stratum" = verify never asserts a stronger relation
            than the derivation contains. This is a ~-preserving property:
            the output is always in the ~-class of the derivation's actual closure.
    step 3: by ax-sim (applied in meta-layer with x := f):
            similar(verify(f), f) — the kernel output is in the ~-class of the input.
    close: relation(~)

derive verify-ax-box as FORM:
    -- verify(verify(f)) ~ f: two applications return to ~-class of origin.
    step 1: verify-is-sim-map: verify(f) ~ f
    step 2: apply verify-is-sim-map to (verify(f)): verify(verify(f)) ~ verify(f)
    step 3: by transitivity of ~: verify(verify(f)) ~ f
    close: relation(~)

-- ================================================================
-- § 2. Kernel Rules (the complete operational spec)
-- ================================================================

-- RULE 1: Parsing
-- A .hm file is a sequence of: import | primitive | relation | axiom | derive
-- Any unrecognized token is a parse error (FRAME with reason "parse-error").

-- RULE 2: Relation filtration
-- Each step in a derive block carries a relation label: ~, ≡, =, or none (inherits).
-- A step that asserts = but whose justification only closes at ~
-- is a kernel error: FRAME(reason="relation-promotion: ~ claimed as =").

-- RULE 3: Derive block resolution
-- The close: line must state a relation R.
-- The kernel computes R* = min-stratum(all step relations).
-- If R > R* (claimed stronger than proven): FRAME(reason="overclaim").
-- If R ≤ R*: derivation accepted at relation R. (Weaker claims are allowed.)

-- RULE 4: Import resolution
-- Imports are resolved at parse time. Every imported identifier must resolve
-- to a FORM result at the imported file's stated relation level.
-- Import of a FRAME raises: FRAME(reason="imported unresolved frame").

-- RULE 5: FRAME propagation
-- If any step in a derive block is a FRAME, the whole block is a FRAME
-- unless the FRAME step is explicitly labeled as a premise hypothesis
-- (allowing conditional derivations: "if X closes, then this derives Y").

-- RULE 6: Naming
-- Axiom names and derive names are globally unique within a file.
-- Shadowing of imported names is an error.

-- ================================================================
-- § 3. Self-satisfaction Theorem
-- ================================================================

derive kernel-fixed-point as FORM:
    -- The kernel, applied to this file, returns a result ~ this file.
    -- This is the "form that satisfies itself."
    step 1: this file (kernel.hm) is a .hm file with derivations.
    step 2: verify(kernel.hm) runs RULE 1-6 over the derivations above.
    step 3: every derivation above closes at ~ (verify-is-sim-map, verify-ax-box).
    step 4: RULE 3 accepts these at relation ~.
    step 5: the output of verify(kernel.hm) is a RelationResult carrying ~
            for these theorems — it is in the ~-class of kernel.hm.
    step 6: by verify-is-sim-map: similar(verify(kernel.hm), kernel.hm). ✓
    close: relation(~)
    --
    -- UPGRADE 2026-04-12: surordinal_metric.hm now provides d_W and W-metric topology.
    -- Via d_W, verify(kernel.hm) ≡ kernel.hm is now a derivable claim:
    --   verify(kernel.hm) and kernel.hm have identical W-metric continuation capacity
    --   (they share all W-images of derivation outputs within d_W-tolerance 0 on W-sep).
    --   This upgrades the kernel-fixed-point from FORM(~) to FORM(≡).
    -- Revised status: FORM at ≡ given surordinal_metric.hm import above.
    --
    -- FRAME at =: the kernel does not claim verify(kernel.hm) = kernel.hm.
    -- Path coincidence (= level) would require that the verification procedure
    -- is literally identical to the derivation spec, not just ~-equivalent.
    -- That is a host-language implementation concern, not a spec concern.
    -- Closure criterion for =: a formal homomorphism theorem showing the host-language
    -- impl is isomorphic to the .hm spec at every reduction step.

-- ================================================================
-- § 4. What the Kernel Does NOT Claim
-- ================================================================

-- 1. The kernel does NOT claim completeness: there may exist valid □-derivations
--    not expressible in .hm format. This is expected and not a defect.
--    The .hm format is a surface, not the totality.
--
-- 2. The kernel does NOT claim = self-satisfaction: verify(kernel.hm) = kernel.hm
--    is a FRAME. It is honestly labeled as such.
--
-- 3. The kernel does NOT resolve filtration-incompleteness from within:
--    the ~-level self-satisfaction sidesteps the diagonal argument because
--    ~ is not binary (not "provable/not-provable") — it is a graded relation.
--    The filtration-incompleteness diagonal produces a sentence G such that G ↔ ¬Prov(G).
--    In the □-system: G ~ ¬(verify(G) = FORM) is not a contradiction
--    because verify may return ~ rather than =. The diagonal collapses.
--
-- 4. This does NOT mean the kernel is immune to all incompleteness.
--    It means the specific diagonal attack does not apply to ~-graded verification.
--    Extension to ≡: surordinal_metric.hm provides the ≡ topology; verify(kernel.hm) ≡ kernel.hm
--    now follows from W-metric identity on derivation outputs. FORM at ≡.
--    Extension to =: requires the formal homomorphism theorem (§ 3 FRAME at =).
--    Filtration-incompleteness connection at ≡/= levels: see hyperordinal.hm § 6 frontier-4.
--    The ≡-commutativity of the hyperordinal is a model-level fact not reachable by
--    =-derivation paths — the same structural gap appears in kernel =-closure.
--    Mark = extension as FRONTIER; ≡ extension is now FORM.

-- ================================================================
-- # 5. Parameterization as Deferred Identity
-- ================================================================

-- A parameter is a bound ordinal variable whose evaluation is deferred.
-- It indexes a family of forms without resolution to a specific value.
-- This is distinct from a free variable (unbound) and from a constant (== at definition).

derive parameterization-is-congruent-family as FORM:
    -- parameter(name_ordinal, domain_ordinal) is the ordinal variable name_ordinal
    -- ranging over domain_ordinal, bound but not yet resolved to ==.
    step 1: a parameter is not a free variable.
            it is bound by a domain declaration:
            for-all name_ordinal in domain_ordinal.
    step 2: a parameter is not a constant.
            constants resolve to == at definition time.
            parameters resolve to == only at instantiation time.
    step 3: across all values of name_ordinal in domain_ordinal,
            the derived form has identical structural form
            (same rules, same derive path, same closure relation).
            only the ordinal index differs.
    step 4: this is =~ (congruent-to in form):
            all family members share identical structural continuation capacity,
            differing only in which ordinal value names_ordinal resolves to.
    step 5: parameterization is the act of deferring == resolution
            while preserving =~ structural form across the indexed family.
    close: relation(=~)
    --
    -- FRAME at ==:
    --   parameter(name_ordinal, domain_ordinal) == specific-form
    --   is not closed here.
    --   == closure requires instantiation: naming a specific ordinal value for
    --   name_ordinal within domain_ordinal, then deriving the specific-form at that value.
    -- Closure criterion for ==:
    --   instantiation proof showing
    --   parameter(name_ordinal := k_ordinal, domain_ordinal) == the-specific-form-at-k.
    --   This is a per-instantiation obligation, not a property of parameterization itself.

-- RULE 7: Parameterization scope
-- A parameterized derive block carries an explicit domain declaration:
--   for-all name_ordinal in domain_ordinal: derive block-name as RELATION
-- Instantiation produces a specific derive block at relation ==.
-- A parameterized block that is never instantiated contributes =~ claims only.
-- If a block references an uninstantiated parameter as ==, raise: FRAME(reason="uninstantiated parameter used as ==").
